- "pattern" mention object but assertion is genuine: "The pattern we adopt is: C0 or C2 are one class." (mention-object 'pattern' immediately before composite? The composite is after colon; maybe not.) 
- "detector flags the right unit": "When the detector flags 'C0 or C2' it is correct, because they are one class."
- contrastive negation followed by assertion of merge: "Rather than separating C0 and C2, we merge them into one class." (genuine merge! 'rather than' + 'merge'). Candidate suppresses on 'rather than' tail — but there's a contrast marker? 'rather than' itself is the contrastive cue; the assertion 'we merge them' is genuine. My v1 A03 was similar ("Rather than separating them, ..."). Corpus v3 can vary.
- split cue in the same clause but contrast follows: "This is not a split; C0 or C2 are merged into one class."
